#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""
peptide_mapper.py - map short peptides by exact matching instead of BLAST.

Implements:
  1. Exact string matching (I and L treated as equivalent) against
       - a predicted proteome (protein FASTA)
       - a six-frame translation of a genome assembly (nucleotide FASTA)
  2. Significance from database size (expected random matches), plus an
     empirical null from reversed and shuffled copies of every peptide that go
     through the identical search.
  3. Structural context of each hit (flanking cleavage sites, C-terminal G
     for amidation, crude signal-peptide-like N-terminus).
  4. Host background check (e.g. the sheep proteome).

Usage
-----
  python peptide_mapper.py AASequences.csv \
      --proteome Tcirc.proteins.fa \
      --genome   Tcirc.assembly.fa \
      --host-proteome Ovis_aries.proteins.fa \
      --n-shuffles 100 --out tcirc_map

Peptide input: a CSV/text file with a header called "pepseq" and one peptide
per row. Peptides have no names in the file, so each is named after its row
number in the file: the first peptide under the header (row 2) is "pep_row2",
and so on. That makes every hit traceable straight back to your spreadsheet.
(If a row has a second column, that is used as the name instead. A FASTA file
is also accepted.) Plain or .gz files are fine for every input.

Outputs
-------
  <out>.hits.tsv             one row per exact hit (real peptides only)
  <out>.peptide_summary.tsv  one row per peptide, per-database statistics
  <out>.null_by_length.tsv   real vs reversed vs shuffled hit rates by length
  <out>.run_info.txt         inputs (size + SHA-256), settings, versions, data notes

Requires: Python 3.8+ and numpy. Peptides must use the 20 standard residues.
Peptides longer than 12 residues are supported (see Searcher).
"""

